    <!-- app-info.xsd defines these as an ORDERED SEQUENCE, not a set. Both the
         children of <dependencies> and the top-level elements below must appear
         in schema order or the file fails validation outright — which is what
         `info.xml lint` was reporting. `<php>` precedes `<nextcloud>`, and the
         top-level order is dependencies, repair-steps, settings, activity,
         navigations (matching openregister and docudesk, which validate). -->
    <dependencies>
        <php min-version="8.3"/>
        <nextcloud min-version="32" max-version="34"/>
        <!-- App dependency (not expressible in app-info.xsd): OpenRegister
             (data layer for the task/project/column/timeEntry/label/dependency
             schemas; see lib/Settings/planninq_register.json). -->
    </dependencies>

    <!-- Schema order inside <repair-steps> is post-migration before install,
         which reads backwards but is what app-info.xsd declares. Order here is
         XML sequence only — it does not change when Nextcloud runs each hook. -->
    <!--
        planix -> planninq ORDERING (applies to BOTH blocks below):
        MigrateAppConfigKeys MUST be listed before InitializeSettings.

        Changing <id> from `planix` to `planninq` does not upgrade this app in
        place — Nextcloud has no notion of an app-id rename, so it simply
        discovers a DIFFERENT app. On a real deployment the old `planix` app is
        removed and `planninq` is installed fresh, which means <install> (not
        just <post-migration>) is the hook this actually runs through; the step
        is registered under both so neither path can miss it.

        Ordering matters because InitializeSettings imports
        lib/Settings/planninq_register.json and calls SettingsService, which
        reads and writes this app's IAppConfig values (register/schema ids, the
        default column config, due-reminder settings). Those values currently
        live in the `planix` app-config namespace. If InitializeSettings ran
        first it would see an unconfigured app, re-import from scratch and
        write fresh `planninq.*` values; MigrateAppConfigKeys never overwrites
        an already-set destination key, so the migrated ones would then be
        silently discarded and the install would point at newly created,
        empty registers instead of the existing data-carrying ones. Copying the
        old namespace across FIRST lets InitializeSettings see the real,
        pre-rename configuration and reconcile against it.

        MigrateAppConfigKeys is idempotent and copy-only (it never deletes a
        `planix.*` row), so it is a safe no-op on a genuinely fresh instance
        that never had `planix` installed.

        MigrateUserPreferences MUST run before ReconcileDueReminderOverrides.
        That step asks `getUsersForUserValue(APP_ID, 'notify_due_reminder',
        'false')` for the users who opted out, and APP_ID is now `planninq` —
        so until the preferences have been copied across the rename it sees an
        empty set and reconciles nothing. Ordering it after the copy is what
        makes the two agree about who opted out.

        MigrateRegisterSlug MUST run after the two app-id steps and before
        InitializeSettings, in BOTH blocks.

        It carries a DIFFERENT rename on a different store: the OpenRegister
        register slug `planix` -> `planninq`, which the app-id rename
        deliberately left behind. OpenRegister's ImportHandler resolves a
        register by SLUG and by nothing else, and its DoesNotExistException
        branch is not an error path — it is the "create a new one" path. So the
        renamed slug in lib/Settings/planninq_register.json renames nothing on
        its own: InitializeSettings triggers the import, the import finds no
        `planninq` register, CREATES A SECOND, EMPTY one, and every stored
        project, task, column, timeEntry, label and dependency stays behind on
        the old row addressed by nothing. Nothing errors — the app just looks
        new. Renaming the row FIRST makes the import recognise it and UPDATE it.

        It moves no data. An object is bound to its register by NUMERIC id: the
        `_register` column holds the id and the shard tables are named
        `oc_openregister_table_<registerId>_<schemaId>`. The slug appears
        nowhere in the physical layout, so this is a one-column UPDATE on one
        row and every object, table, schema link and folder follows it
        untouched.

        AFTER MigrateAppConfigKeys for a second reason: that step copies the
        whole `planix` app-config namespace across VERBATIM, so a stored
        `register` value naming the old slug arrives under `planninq` intact.
        MigrateRegisterSlug re-points it — guarded on the VALUE, so the numeric
        register id this app normally stores there is never touched.

        Idempotent, non-destructive, and it never throws: under <install> an
        escaping exception aborts the install and the app never enables at all.
        It refuses rather than merges when both slugs already exist, because
        OpenRegister caps a slug lookup at one row ordered by id and the lower
        id would silently win.
    -->
    <repair-steps>
        <post-migration>
            <step>OCA\Planninq\Repair\MigrateAppConfigKeys</step>
            <step>OCA\Planninq\Repair\MigrateUserPreferences</step>
            <step>OCA\Planninq\Repair\MigrateRegisterSlug</step>
            <!--
                ONE LEVEL DOWN FROM MigrateRegisterSlug, AND THE SAME TRAP.
                `timeEntry` is a slug three apps declared — humaniq's HR
                booking, pipelinq's billing record and this app's project
                booking — and a slug is global per organisation, so
                SchemaMapper::find() answered whichever row it reached first.
                humaniq owns the hours; this record is the PLANNING side of one,
                and keeps a `timeEntry` reference to the humaniq booking.

                Renaming the row BEFORE InitializeSettings is what makes it a
                rename: the import matches by (application, slug) and its
                not-found branch CREATES a second schema, orphaning every
                existing entry silently while the app reads an empty list.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Planninq\Repair\RenameTimeEntrySchemaSlug</step>
            <step>OCA\Planninq\Repair\InitializeSettings</step>
            <step>OCA\Planninq\Repair\ReconcileDueReminderOverrides</step>
        </post-migration>
        <install>
            <step>OCA\Planninq\Repair\MigrateAppConfigKeys</step>
            <step>OCA\Planninq\Repair\MigrateUserPreferences</step>
            <!--
                THE BLOCK THAT ACTUALLY RUNS. Nextcloud discovers `planninq` as
                a DIFFERENT app from `planix`, so enabling it IS a fresh install
                with an empty `installed_version` —
                `Installer::installAppLastSteps()` guards both the pre- and
                post-migration blocks with `if ($previousVersion !== '')`, and
                only `repair-steps/install` executes. The register row created
                by the previous app id is still there, so the rename has to
                happen here or the import below forks a second, empty register.
            -->
            <step>OCA\Planninq\Repair\MigrateRegisterSlug</step>
            <!-- See the note in <post-migration>. -->
            <step>OCA\Planninq\Repair\RenameTimeEntrySchemaSlug</step>
            <step>OCA\Planninq\Repair\InitializeSettings</step>
        </install>
    </repair-steps>

    <settings>
        <admin>OCA\Planninq\Settings\AdminSettings</admin>
        <admin-section>OCA\Planninq\Sections\SettingsSection</admin-section>
    </settings>

    <!-- Schema order inside <activity> is settings, filters, providers. -->
    <activity>
        <filters>
            <filter>OCA\Planninq\Activity\Filter</filter>
        </filters>
        <providers>
            <provider>OCA\Planninq\Activity\Provider</provider>
        </providers>
    </activity>

